# This is a tutorial for setting up GitHub with SSH
# For Linux users

1. Check fot Existing Keys:
	$ ls -al ~/.ssh

- If you see files like id_rsa.pub, id_ed25519, or id_ecdsa.pub, you already have a key.


2. Generate a new SSH key:
	$ ssh-keygen -t ed2519 -C "your-email@x.com"

- you have to add your github email.
- you can press Enter to save it in the default location.
- you can add a password or leave it blank.


3. Add your key to the ssh agent:
- This ensures your computer remember the key so you don't have to re-enter your passphrase constantly.
	$ eval "$(ssh-agent -s)"	--> To start the agent
	$ ssh-add ~/.ssh/id_ed25519	--> Add your key


4. Add public key to Github:

	$ cat ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub	--> Copy the output of this.

- Go to GitHub Settings --> SSh and GPG keys --> New SSH key --> Give it a name --> Paste 


5. Test the Connection:
	$ ssh -T git@github.com

- You will see a warning about the authenticity of the host. --> Type yes


	
